---

## 1. Core Mechanics

Quicksort is a **comparison-based, divide-and-conquer, in-place sorting algorithm** developed by C. A. R. Hoare in 1959. Its central operation is **partitioning**: rearranging an array around a chosen **pivot** so that every element less than or equal to the pivot precedes it, and every element greater than it follows it. The pivot then occupies its final, sorted position. The algorithm recurses on the left and right sub-arrays.

### 1.1 The Partition Operation

Two partitioning schemes dominate the literature: **Lomuto** and **Hoare**.

#### Lomuto Partition Scheme
This is the scheme most commonly taught because of its simple implementation.

```text
Partition(A, low, high):
    pivot := A[high]                 // choose last element as pivot
    i := low - 1                     // boundary of the "≤ pivot" region
    
    for j := low to high - 1:
        if A[j] <= pivot:
            i := i + 1
            swap(A[i], A[j])'
