2026-11-02 09:03:06 - [INFO] - Jarvis.ConversationManager - [MainThread] - [CONVERSATION] Response (10115.3ms): 'Sir, the working set model is a method the operating system uses to decide how much memory each program genuinely needs at any moment.

It rests on the idea of locality: even a large program tends to touch only a small group of pages or data over a short stretch of time.

The OS watches a moving window of recent memory references—for example, the last few thousand instructions or a fixed slice of time. Every distinct page referenced inside that window forms the process’s working set.

If the OS allocates the process at least as many frames as its working set size, page faults remain low and the program runs smoothly. If it allocates fewer frames than the working set requires, the process constantly swaps pages in and out of memory—that is thrashing.

To prevent this, the OS adds together the working sets of all running programs. If their combined size fits in available RAM, all is well. If it exceeds memory, the OS suspends or swaps out some programs to free frames for the others.

