2026-11-02 09:11:11 - [INFO] - Jarvis.ConversationManager - [MainThread] - [CONVERSATION] Response (8442.4ms): 'Sir, imagine you have three servers working together, and they need to agree on who is in charge.

**Split brain** is what happens when the network breaks in half. One server ends up on one side, and two servers end up on the other. Neither side can talk to the other. Without a rule to stop them, both sides might decide, “I’m the leader now!” and start accepting different instructions. When the network finally reconnects, you have two conflicting sets of data and no idea which one is correct. That is split brain.

**A quorum** is simply a majority—more than half. In a three-server cluster, a quorum is two servers. In a five-server cluster, it is three.

Here is how the quorum prevents split brain:

Before any server can declare itself leader or accept an important change, it must get agreement from a quorum. Because a quorum is *more than half* of the total, it is mathematically impossible for two separate groups to each have a quorum at the same time.

Going back to our three-server example: if the network splits into a group of two and a group of one, only the group of two has a quorum. That side can elect a leader and keep working. The lone server on the other side cannot reach a quorum, so it must step down and wait. It cannot act as leader, and it cannot accept writes.

Think of it like a jury. A verdict needs a majority of jurors. If the jury is accidentally split into two rooms, only the room with more than half the jurors can reach a verdict. The people in the smaller room cannot declare one on their own.

So, sir, the quorum ensures that **only one side of a broken cluster can ever make decisions**. The other side stays quiet until the network is repaired, preventing conflicting data and keeping the system consistent.'
